### How to Optimize

#### 1. Optimal Solution: Kadane’s Algorithm — $O(N)$ Time
Instead of re-calculating sums for all subarrays, you can iterate through the array **once**:
1. Keep a running `sum` of the current subarray.
2. Update the maximum sum seen so far (`maxi`).
3. If `sum` becomes negative, reset it to `0` (since a negative sum will only decrease the total of any future subarray).

#### 2. C++ Cleanups
* Replace `<bits/stdc++.h>` with specific headers (`<iostream>`, `<vector>`, `<algorithm>`) to reduce compile time.
* Pass `const vector<int>& nums` to avoid unnecessary modification/copy overhead.
* Use `'\n'` instead of `endl` to prevent unnecessary buffer flushes.

---

### Optimized Code ($O(N)$ Time, $O(1)$ Space)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

class Solution {
public:
    int maxSubArray(const std::vector<int>& nums) {
        int maxi = nums[0];
        int sum = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            sum += num;
            maxi = std::max(maxi, sum);

            // If sum drops below 0, reset it
            if (sum < 0) {
                sum = 0;
            }
        }

        return maxi;
    }
};

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4};

    Solution obj;
    std::cout << obj.maxSubArray(nums) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

